//! The `break-before` [CSS](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS) property sets how
//! page, column, or region breaks should behave before a generated box. If there is no generated
//! box, the property is ignored.
//!
//! Initial: auto. Inherited: no.
//! Percentages: not accepted.
//!
//! Keyword effects:
//! - all: Forces a page break right before the principal box. Breaking through all possible
//! fragmentation contexts. So a break inside a multicol container, which was inside a page container
//! would force a column and page break.
//! - always: Forces a page break right before the principal box. The type of this break is that of
//! the immediately-containing fragmentation context. If we are inside a multicol container then it
//! would force a column break, inside paged media (but not inside a multicol container) a page
//! break.
//! - auto: Allows, but does not force, any break (page, column, or region) to be inserted right
//! before the principal box.
//! - avoid: Avoids any break (page, column, or region) from being inserted right before the
//! principal box.
//! - avoid-column: Avoids any column break right before the principal box.
//! - avoid-page: Avoids any page break right before the principal box.
//! - avoid-region: Avoids any region break right before the principal box.
//! - column: Forces a column break right before the principal box.
//! - left: Forces one or two page breaks right before the principal box, whichever will make the
//! next page into a left page. It's the page placed on the left side of the spine of the book or the
//! back side of the page in duplex printing.
//! - page: Forces a page break right before the principal box.
//! - recto: Forces one or two page breaks right before the principal box, whichever will make the
//! next page into a recto page. (A recto page is a right page in a left-to-right spread or a left
//! page in a right-to-left spread.)
//! - region: Forces a region break right before the principal box.
//! - right: Forces one or two page breaks right before the principal box, whichever will make the
//! next page into a right page. It's the page placed on the right side of the spine of the book or
//! the front side of the page in duplex printing.
//! - verso: Forces one or two page breaks right before the principal box, whichever will make the
//! next page into a verso page. (A verso page is a left page in a left-to-right spread or a right
//! page in a right-to-left spread.)
//!
//! Grammar: auto | avoid | always | all | avoid-page | page | left | right | recto | verso |
//! avoid-column | column | avoid-region | region
//!
//! Specification: https://drafts.csswg.org/css-break-4/#propdef-break-before
//!
//! Author guide: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/break-before
//!
//! Purpose and value descriptions adapted from MDN contributors (CC-BY-SA-2.5).
//!
//! Generated from @webref/css 8.7.8. Do not edit.

module std.css.properties.BreakBefore.

pub struct Property {
    break_before: std.css.keywords.Auto | std.css.keywords.Avoid | std.css.keywords.Always | std.css.keywords.All | std.css.keywords.AvoidPage | std.css.keywords.Page | std.css.keywords.Left | std.css.keywords.Right | std.css.keywords.Recto | std.css.keywords.Verso | std.css.keywords.AvoidColumn | std.css.keywords.Column | std.css.keywords.AvoidRegion | std.css.keywords.Region | std.css.Values.Inherit | std.css.Values.Initial | std.css.Values.Unset | std.css.Values.Revert | std.css.Values.RevertLayer
}.
